{:edb/db-before-t 1663 :edb/db-after-t 1664 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534976 50 #inst "2026-10-08T12:51:10.299Z" 13194139534976 true] [17592186195177 1104 #edb/ref 17592186195180 13194139534976 true] [17592186195182 1104 #edb/ref 17592186195185 13194139534976 true] [17592186195289 1104 #edb/ref 17592186195293 13194139534976 true] [17592186195295 1104 #edb/ref 17592186195298 13194139534976 true] [17592186195301 1104 #edb/ref 17592186195306 13194139534976 true] [17592186198402 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324145 13194139534976 true] [17592186198402 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324157 13194139534976 true] [17592186198404 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324137 13194139534976 true] [17592186198701 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534976 true] [17592186198701 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044982/assets/5c4ffbb64a754df9a6100fda3fd6eaf4a68f28ac59de4dfb716c2c8b1f2847ab.png)\n\nUse set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram above:\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,y\\,{{field-2}}\\,{x}^{2}}\n$$" 13194139534976 true] [17592186198701 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4392/Source/Images/q-181172.png)\n\nUse set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram above:\n\n$R$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}" 13194139534976 false] [17592186198701 1145 "The points in our set include all those that lie *below* the curve $y={x}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, a point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\ny<{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nWe must also include the curve $y={x}^{2}$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\ny\\le {x}^{2}\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\le {x}^{2}}.\n$$" 13194139534976 true] [17592186198715 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534976 true] [17592186198715 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044982/assets/83ebd6f5d742a869b267a0cead4a9a94ae17fa534ca3e1cfddbb495c67390ecf.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,-x\\,{{field-2}}\\,y\\,{{field-3}}\\,2}\n$$" 13194139534976 true] [17592186198715 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4392/Source/Images/q-181600.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$R$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}} {{field-3}}" 13194139534976 false] [17592186198715 1145 "The points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=-x$ and *below* the line $y=2.$\n\nA point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\ny>-x,\\,y<2.\n$$\n\nCombining this into a single condition, we have\n\n$$\n-x<y<2.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the line $y=-x$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the corresponding inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\n-x\\le y<2\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-x\\le y<2}.\n$$" 13194139534976 true] [17592186324137 1017 #edb/ref 17592186324138 13194139534976 true] [17592186324137 1017 #edb/ref 17592186324141 13194139534976 true] [17592186324137 1017 #edb/ref 17592186324143 13194139534976 true] [17592186324137 1089 "q-181694" 13194139534976 true] [17592186324137 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534976 true] [17592186324137 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044982/assets/c670ed592fc32716596aae470911923627d1c6861a75a1c885659d09005e0a84.png)\n\nUse set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram above:\n\n${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n${x}^{2}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n${y}^{2}$\n{{field-3}}\n\n$36$\n\n$}$" 13194139534976 true] [17592186324137 1113 #uuid "b4e1ca48-5085-5e98-9dc5-2438b5edfafe" 13194139534976 true] [17592186324137 1145 "The equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the elliptical boundary of our region has horizontal radius $a=2$ and vertical radius $b=3.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=36.\n$$\n\nWe're interested in describing all points that lie *inside* the ellipse. So, let's pick any point inside the region, say, $(0,0).$ Substituting its coordinates into the equation of the boundary, we obtain\n\n$$\n9(0)^{2}+4(0)^{2}\\,<\\,36.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the points $P(x,y)$ in our region are any real numbers that satisfy the condition\n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}<36.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the curve $9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=36$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\le 36.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\le 36}.\n$$" 13194139534976 true] [17592186324138 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324139 13194139534976 true] [17592186324138 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324140 13194139534976 true] [17592186324138 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534976 true] [17592186324138 1041 "field-1" 13194139534976 true] [17592186324138 1104 #edb/ref 17592186324139 13194139534976 true] [17592186324138 1110 #uuid "44750a13-f5d9-5ed1-ba24-90bf45e702b7" 13194139534976 true] [17592186324139 1034 "9" 13194139534976 true] [17592186324139 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 131